@* Toolbar's Actions page, moved from its Overview: form posts, OnClick, toggle buttons and split buttons. *@

<Heading Level="2">Posting a form</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toolbar button can post the form around the toolbar, as a <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor> does. Set <Code>Submit</Code>, and set <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code> to tell the actions apart. In the … menu, the button posts the same, with <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">JavaScript off</Anchor> too.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-bulk-actions" />

<Heading Level="2">Running C# on click</Heading>
<TextBlock>On interactive pages, a toolbar button can run C# when clicked. Set <Code>OnClick</Code>. It runs whether the user clicks the button in the bar or in the … menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-on-click" />

<Heading Level="2">Toggle buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A toggle button turns a tool on and off, such as bold text. Bind its state with <Code>@@bind-Pressed</Code>. In the … menu, it becomes a checkbox item that toggles the same state.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-toggles" />

<Heading Level="2">Split buttons</Heading>
<TextBlock>A split button is a main action with a menu of related ones beside it. Use <Code>ToolbarSplitButton</Code>; see <Anchor Href="docs/components/split-button">SplitButton</Anchor>. In the … menu, it becomes a submenu named by its <Code>MenuLabel</Code>, with the main action as the first item.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="toolbar-split-button" />
